From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Bryan Hoban <bryan.hoban@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 13:15:11 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] kodi: keep a Dolby Vision picture on screen when it is asked
 for again

Nothing compared a picture with the one already presented: a pause ran
decode-surface import, reconstruction, packing and an atomic commit at
display rate, and a 24p title on a 60 Hz mode did two and a half times
the GPU work it needed.

The renderer now numbers every picture it queues, and the adapter
remembers the picture on screen for as long as it is also the last one
given to the engine. When the same queued picture comes again and a
frame built from it would be the last frame over again, nothing is
submitted and nothing committed: the buffer on screen is that picture.
"The same frame" is decided by building the frame as Render() would and
comparing it member by member with the one submitted (surfaces, plan,
metadata bytes, output mode), with these rules:

- No overlay now, in the frame on screen or in the one before it: the
  engine marks overlay edges in the metadata, so the frame after an
  overlay is not a repeat yet.
- No refresh owed by the scanout slots (after a rejected commit or a
  stream reset the next picture must carry one). CB1's refresh rule
  already gives a repeated picture the value it had.
- The packet id is not compared. With CB1's processing the id advances
  only when the payload changes, so a repeat has the same id and the
  held buffer is byte for byte what a new one would be. yblod's own
  processing would send the next id with an identical payload; the held
  buffer sends the same id again, which is CB1's rule.
- Never in HDR10+ output: a picture's packet carries the statistics of
  the picture before it until its own are measured, so the second
  rendering of a picture is the one with its own statistics.
- Only while the scanout is stable: a pending mode or signal change
  needs its commit.

A held picture counts as presented for the end-of-stream and startup
waits (CDVPrivateDrain), keeps what the playback info shows, and leaves
the slot bookkeeping untouched. The record goes with every engine
submission, reset and display handover, so a picture is only ever held
against the frame that is really on screen.

The main loop is paced by the blocking commit. For a held picture the
window waits for the next vertical blank instead (the last blank's
time plus a refresh period, checked against the CRTC's count). The
same branch returned at once whenever nothing was presented, before
the first picture, for a picture the engine does not take, after an
engine failure and during the retry delay of a rejected commit, and
spun a core; it now pauses 10 ms as its sibling branches do.

DV_PRIVATE_SKIP_REPEATS=0 renders every picture as before.
